            <h1>How to Knock in a Cricket Bat (Step-by-Step)</h1>
            
            <div class="summary-box">
                <h3>Direct Answer</h3>
                <p>Knocking in compresses soft willow fibers to prevent cracks. Apply 1 teaspoon of raw linseed oil, let it dry flat for 24 hours, then gently strike the face and edges with a wooden bat mallet for 4–6 hours before taking it to match play.</p>
            </div>

            <h2>Why Knocking In Is Essential</h2>
            <p>Raw English and Kashmir willow is soft. If you hit a hard leather cricket ball with an un-knocked bat, the wood will seam, crack, or split instantly. Knocking in compresses the wood fibers and creates a resilient surface layer that absorbs high-speed impacts.</p>

            <h2>Required Supplies</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Raw Linseed Oil (do not use boiled linseed oil)</li>
                <li>Soft microfiber cloth</li>
                <li>Wooden cricket bat mallet</li>
                <li>Old leather cricket balls for net testing</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>4 Steps to Prepare Your Bat</h2>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Oil the Face:</strong> Apply a light coat of raw linseed oil to the face, edges, and toe using your cloth. Avoid the handle and splice.</li>
                <li><strong>Dry Horizontally:</strong> Place the bat flat on a horizontal surface for 24 hours to let the oil absorb evenly.</li>
                <li><strong>Mallet Work:</strong> Strike the face lightly with a wooden mallet, slowly increasing force over 4 to 6 hours. Pay special attention to the edges, rounding them gently off at a 45-degree angle.</li>
                <li><strong>Net Practice:</strong> Take the bat to throw-downs using old, soft cricket balls before facing seamers with brand-new match balls.</li>
            </ol>
